---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = "How to Publish Your Website App to the Google Play Store";
const pageDescription = "Step-by-step: build your website app, sign the APK, and submit to Google Play Console. Free, no-code.";

const breadcrumb = {
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Guides", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/guides" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "How to Publish Your Website App to the Google Play Store" }
  ]
};

const guideSchema = {
  "@graph": [{
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to publish a website wrapper app to the Google Play Store",
  "description": "Publish the Android APK generated by SiteToApp to the Google Play Store: build, sign, create a Play Console account, upload the release, fill the store listing, and submit for review.",
  "step": [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Build your app APK with SiteToApp", "text": "Enter your website URL on the SiteToApp Android builder, configure the app name, icon, splash screen, and features, then compile to download a signed-ready APK." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Install and test the APK", "text": "Transfer the APK to your Android device and install it. Verify load speed, pull-to-refresh, and offline fallback all work before submitting." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Create a Google Play Console developer account", "text": "Register at play.google.com/console with a one-time $25 verification fee, then complete the identity and app-access verification." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Create the app and upload the release", "text": "Create a new app in Play Console, upload your APK as the first production release, and set your target API and release notes." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Complete the store listing", "text": "Add your app name, short and full description, screenshots from the device preview, feature graphic, privacy policy URL, and content rating questionnaire." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Submit for review and publish", "text": "Run the pre-launch report, submit the release for Google's review (usually hours to a few days), then publish to production." }
  ]
}, breadcrumb]};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} bodyClass="landing-page" structuredData={guideSchema}>
  <main class="shell">
    <article class="content-panel">
      <h1>How to Publish Your Website App to the Google Play Store</h1>
      <p>You've built your Android wrapper APK with SiteToApp — now let's get it live on the Play Store. This guide walks every step, from testing on a real device to hitting <strong>Start rollout</strong> in Play Console. No coding required. If you haven't built your app yet, start at the <a href="/android">Android builder</a>.</p>

      <h2>Before you start: a quick checklist</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your APK downloaded and tested on at least one real Android device</li>
        <li>An app icon (512×512 PNG) and a feature graphic (1024×500 PNG)</li>
        <li>At least two phone screenshots captured from the device preview</li>
        <li>A short description (≤80 chars) and a full description of your app</li>
        <li>A link to your privacy policy page — every Play Store app needs one (<a href="/privacy">use ours as a reference</a>)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 1 — Build and download your APK</h2>
      <p>Open the <a href="/android">Android builder</a>, enter your website URL, app name, package ID (e.g. <code>com.yourbrand.app</code>), and icon. Enable the features you need — pull-to-refresh, splash screen, loading spinner, fullscreen mode — then click <em>Build Android APK</em>. After a short compile, download the APK directly or grab the full workspace ZIP if you want to build and sign it yourself in Android Studio.</p>
      <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> choose a memorable, all-lowercase package ID like <code>com.coffeeshop.app</code>. You can never change it after publishing.</p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Test on a real device</h2>
      <p>Before submitting, install the APK on your Android phone: go to <strong>Settings → Security</strong> and enable <strong>Install unknown apps</strong> for your browser or file manager, then tap the downloaded APK. Run through these checks:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Does the site load correctly and quickly?</li>
        <li>Does the splash screen appear and dismiss cleanly?</li>
        <li>Does pull-to-refresh work?</li>
        <li>Does the back button behave as expected?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If anything looks off, fix it in the builder and recompile — it takes under a minute.</p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Create a Google Play Console developer account</h2>
      <p>Go to <a href="https://play.google.com/console" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">play.google.com/console</a> and register. There is a one-time <strong>$25 USD fee</strong> (charged by Google, not SiteToApp). You'll need a valid payment method, identity verification, and a contact email. Approval typically arrives within one to two business days.</p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Create the app and upload your release</h2>
      <p>Inside Play Console, click <strong>Create app</strong>. Choose your app's name, default language, type (App, not Game), and pricing (Free). Then navigate to <strong>Production → Releases → Create new release → Upload</strong> and add your APK. Play Console will flag the target API level — the SiteToApp wrapper follows current Android guidelines, so this step is straightforward.</p>
      <p>Write clear release notes — even a first release benefits from a short sentence like <em>"Initial release."</em></p>

      <h2>Step 5 — Complete the store listing</h2>
      <p>This is the section most people spend the most time on — and rightly so, it directly affects discoverability. You'll need:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A <strong>short description</strong> (80 characters max) — your elevator pitch</li>
        <li>A <strong>full description</strong> — describe what the app does, who it's for, and what makes it unique</li>
        <li>At least <strong>2 phone screenshots</strong> — use the device preview on the builder page to capture them</li>
        <li>A <strong>1024×500 feature graphic</strong> — shown in search results</li>
        <li>Your <strong>512×512 app icon</strong></li>
        <li>Content rating questionnaire answers</li>
        <li>Data safety form — a SiteToApp wrapper collects no personal data, which simplifies this considerably</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 6 — Submit for review and go live</h2>
      <p>Run Play Console's built-in <strong>pre-launch report</strong> to catch crashes on test devices, then click <strong>Submit for review</strong>. Most wrapper apps clear review within a few hours to a couple of days. Once approved, click <strong>Start rollout to production</strong> and your app goes live on the Play Store worldwide.</p>

      <h2>Keeping your app fresh — without rebuilding</h2>
      <p>Because the wrapper loads your live website inside a WebView, any content change on your site shows up in the app automatically — no new APK needed. You only need to recompile when you change the app name, icon, package ID, or native features like splash screen or pull-to-refresh.</p>

      <p>Ready to build? <a href="/android"><strong>Open the free Android builder</strong></a>, or check the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> for answers on Play Protect warnings, signing, and publishing costs.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
